    "knowledge_point": "Simplifying an Expression by Factoring and Using Various Forms of the Secant-Tangent Identity",
    "problem": "Which of the following is equivalent to ${tan}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ?$",
    "worked_solution": "First, we recall the different forms of the secant-tangent Identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & (1) & 1+{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta ={sec}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & (2) & {tan}^{2}⁡\\theta ={sec}^{2}⁡\\theta -1 \\\\  & (3) & {sec}^{2}⁡\\theta -{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can rewrite our expression using $(1),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{tan}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,({tan}^{2}⁡\\theta +1) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can further simplify and use $(2),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {sec}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & ={tan}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =({sec}^{2}⁡\\theta -1)\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{tan}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =({sec}^{2}⁡\\theta -1)\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta .\n$$",
